# Engineering discipline in {{repository}}

Written by `praxis adopt`. This file is a **pointer**, not a copy. It names the plugin skill
that holds each rule, then states the handful of facts that are true of this repository and
of no other. When the two disagree, the plugin skill wins — and if you find yourself
restating a rule here, you have just created the second copy this whole method exists to
prevent.

The version this replaced was sixteen skill templates and eleven prompts copied into the
host repository, every one of them describing a spine the method had already deleted. The
doctrine below survived that deletion on its own merits: it is about **code**, and none of
it was ever bound to how work was tracked.

---

## Structure — capability-driven, not layer-driven

Read `design-capability-layout`. The short version, so you know whether you need it:

Code is organised by **what the system can do**, not by what kind of thing a file is. A
capability owns its own vertical slice — its domain logic, its ports, its adapters, its
tests — and a change to one capability touches one directory.

**There are no dumping grounds.** `utils/`, `helpers/`, `common/`, `shared/`, `misc/` are
refused by `scripts/check-anti-dumping.sh`, which runs in `verify.sh` and in the pre-commit
hook. A file that genuinely belongs to several capabilities belongs to a named one of them,
or it is a capability nobody has named yet.

Inside a capability, the **functional core** is pure and the **imperative shell** does the
I/O. The core is where the decisions are and the shell is where the world is, and the reason
to keep them apart is that only one of the two is cheap to test.

**In this repository:**

- Capabilities live under `{{source-root}}`
- The language is `{{language}}`

